From 1c2057054345f62d9b5e54ff734241137b31b572 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: orlfman <25542856+orlfman@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 17:03:53 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 17/17] linux: harden Intel iGPU sampling for xe and the GPU%
 column

Runtime power: reading xe client cycle counters or freq0 restarts the
GPU's autosuspend timer, so a fast htop refresh kept an idle xe iGPU
awake. On xe, the clock and client counters are now read at most once
per autosuspend_delay_ms plus a quarter of it (the kernel's timer
slack) plus 50 ms, reusing the previous sample in between; sleep state
is still checked on every refresh, and power/control=on reads every
refresh. A "resuming" runtime status now shows as sleep instead of N/A,
on i915 as well.

GPU% column: the generic process reader only understands drm-engine-*
nanoseconds, while xe reports cycles, and reading an xe descriptor's
fdinfo is what wakes the GPU. Descriptors whose device node is bound to
xe are skipped before their fdinfo is read.

Descriptor identification: following a /proc fd link with stat() asks
the target's own filesystem, which can block on a hung NFS, SMB or FUSE
mount. Links are now classified from their text first. Non-paths are
never devices; /dev/dri nodes are looked up; other paths only when they
resolve to an allowlisted local filesystem, using this process's
mountinfo resolved by mount and parent ID (one parse per refresh,
shared by both readers). Anything else is identified by its fdinfo in
the Intel sampler, which never reaches the file's filesystem, so DRM
nodes under other names (containers, overlay) are still counted. The
lookup uses the raw statx system call with cached attributes when
available, falls back to fstatat/stat when the kernel or a seccomp
policy rejects it, and builds without openat, readlinkat or fstatat use
path-based calls.

Co-Authored-By: OpenAI Codex (GPT-6.2 Astra) <noreply@openai.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
